# 28. Update the Application

Suppose you change:

```html
<h1>Hello from Kubernetes!</h1>
```

to:

```html
<h1>Kubernetes Deployment Updated!</h1>
```

Build a new Docker image:

```bash
docker build -t kubernetes-web-app:v2 .
```

Then update the Deployment:

```bash
kubectl set image deployment/kubernetes-web-deployment web-container=kubernetes-web-app:v2
```

Check:

```bash
kubectl rollout status deployment/kubernetes-web-deployment
```

Expected:

```text
deployment "kubernetes-web-deployment" successfully rolled out
```

---

# 29. Check the Updated Pods

Run:

```bash
kubectl get pods
```

Kubernetes will gradually replace the old Pods with Pods running the new image.

This is called a **rolling update**.

---

# 30. View Deployment History

Run:

```bash
kubectl rollout history deployment/kubernetes-web-deployment
```

This allows you to see previous Deployment revisions.

---

# 31. Roll Back a Deployment

If the new version has a problem:

```bash
kubectl rollout undo deployment/kubernetes-web-deployment
```

Check:

```bash
kubectl rollout status deployment/kubernetes-web-deployment
```

Kubernetes rolls back to the previous revision.

